{
  "title": "Sickling: flexibility, flow and distinct crises",
  "sources": [
    "CH30S23",
    "CH30S24",
    "CH30S26",
    "CH30S27",
    "CH30S29"
  ],
  "stages": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "title": "Flexible red cells",
      "text": "A red cell must deform to move through microcirculation. The normal cell and the sickle cell shown here are adapted from scholarly illustrations. The motion uses schematic time and scale."
    },
    {
      "start": 11.1,
      "title": "Stiffening and obstruction",
      "text": "In sickle cell disease, abnormal hemoglobin polymerization stiffens the cell. Hypoxia, dehydration and acidosis favor sickling in the packet. Stiff cells obstruct small vessels, causing pain and tissue injury."
    },
    {
      "start": 24.58,
      "title": "Different crisis mechanisms",
      "text": "Keep the crises distinct. Vasoocclusive crisis obstructs microcirculation. Aplastic crisis transiently stops production. Sequestration crisis pools blood in the spleen. Hyperhemolytic crisis increases red cell destruction."
    },
    {
      "start": 39.58,
      "title": "Mechanism guides the decision",
      "text": "The treatment slide links care to crisis prevention, pain and infection management, fluids and hydroxyurea. Supplemental oxygen is qualified by current hypoxia. Chapter thirty, slides twenty three, twenty four, twenty six, twenty seven and twenty nine, supply these claims. The contradictory amino acid substitution wording is excluded; definitive diagnostic testing remains missing."
    }
  ],
